05},[99,1732,1733],{"class":109}," owned ",[99,1735,113],{"class":105},[99,1737,1738],{"class":105}," *",[99,1740,1741],{"class":109},"b; ",[99,1743,1744],{"class":136},"\u002F\u002F unbox (moves String out)\n",[23,1746,1748],{"id":1747},"tips-tricks","💡 Tips & Tricks",[62,1750,1751,1772,1788,1804,1818,1829],{},[31,1752,1753,721,1756,1759,1760,1762,1763,1767,1768,1771],{},[18,1754,1755],{},"Debug",[53,1757,1758],{},"dbg!(&value)"," prints the file, line, and a ",[53,1761,1755],{}," dump of an expression ",[1764,1765,1766],"em",{},"and"," returns ownership of it, so you can splice it into a move chain: ",[53,1769,1770],{},"let s = dbg!(String::from(\"hi\"));"," without disturbing the move.",[31,1773,1774,1777,1778,739,1781,1784,1785,1787],{},[18,1775,1776],{},"Idiom",": prefer ",[53,1779,1780],{},"std::mem::take",[53,1782,1783],{},"std::mem::replace"," over ",[53,1786,373],{}," when you need to \"empty out\" a field during a state transition — it's O(1) and avoids an allocation.",[31,1789,1790,721,1793,739,1796,1799,1800,1803],{},[18,1791,1792],{},"Performance",[53,1794,1795],{},"Rc::clone(&rc)",[53,1797,1798],{},"Arc::clone(&arc)"," never deep-copy; the call is a refcount bump. Writing ",[53,1801,1802],{},"rc.clone()"," compiles to the same thing but reads ambiguously — always use the explicit associated-function form in shared code.",[31,1805,1806,721,1808,1811,1812,1814,1815,1817],{},[18,1807,1776],{},[53,1809,1810],{},"Box\u003CT>"," values move just like any other value — a move of a ",[53,1813,76],{}," is a pointer-sized copy of the box header, not a deep copy of the heap data, which is why moving even a gigabyte-sized ",[53,1816,80],{}," is cheap.",[31,1819,1820,721,1822,1825,1826,1828],{},[18,1821,1755],{},[53,1823,1824],{},"std::mem::size_of::\u003CT>()"," is handy for confirming your intuition about move cost — ",[53,1827,83],{}," is 24 bytes on 64-bit regardless of how much heap data it owns.",[31,1830,1831,721,1834,1837,1838,1841],{},[18,1832,1833],{},"Clippy",[53,1835,1836],{},"clippy::redundant_clone"," flags ",[53,1839,1840],{},".clone()"," calls whose result is never used mutably alongside the original — a common sign a borrow would have worked instead.",[23,1843,1845],{"id":1844},"️-edge-cases-gotchas","⚠️ Edge Cases & Gotchas",[62,1847,1848,1868,1897,1913,1931,1943,1959],{},[31,1849,1850,721,1853,1856,1857,1859,1860,1863,1864,1867],{},[18,1851,1852],{},"Moving into a loop condition",[53,1854,1855],{},"for s in vec_of_strings"," consumes the ",[53,1858,80],{}," — ",[53,1861,1862],{},"vec_of_strings"," is gone after the loop. Use ",[53,1865,1866],{},"&vec_of_strings"," to iterate by reference and keep ownership.",[31,1869,1870,721,1879,1882,1883,1886,1887,1889,1890,1893,1894,1896],{},[18,1871,1872,739,1875,1878],{},[53,1873,1874],{},"if",[53,1876,1877],{},"else"," branches move differently",[53,1880,1881],{},"let x = if cond { a } else { b };"," moves whichever branch runs; the compiler tracks this per-branch, so using ",[53,1884,1885],{},"a"," after the ",[53,1888,1874],{}," (when ",[53,1891,1892],{},"cond"," was true) is still an error even though the ",[53,1895,1877],{}," branch \"didn't touch it.\"",[31,1898,1899,721,1902,1905,1906,1908,1909,1912],{},[18,1900,1901],{},"Shadowing doesn't drop early",[53,1903,1904],{},"let s = String::from(\"a\"); let s = String::from(\"b\");"," — the first ",[53,1907,728],{}," isn't dropped when shadowed; it drops at the ",[1764,1910,1911],{},"original"," scope's end (after the second one, in reverse declaration order), which can surprise you when both hold expensive resources.",[31,1914,1915,1920,1921,1923,1924,1927,1928,1930],{},[18,1916,1917,1919],{},[53,1918,55],{}," closures silently duplicate, not move",": a closure that captures a ",[53,1922,55],{}," type by value takes a copy each call if it's ",[53,1925,1926],{},"Fn",", so mutations inside the closure never propagate back to the caller — this is invisible without checking the captured type's ",[53,1929,55],{},"-ness.",[31,1932,1933,721,1939,1942],{},[18,1934,1935,1936],{},"Self-assignment through ",[53,1937,1938],{},"mem::swap",[53,1940,1941],{},"std::mem::swap(&mut a, &mut a)"," is legal but a no-op — the borrow checker allows it because it's a single mutable borrow, not two, but it wastes a swap's worth of moves.",[31,1944,1945,1950,1951,1954,1955,1958],{},[18,1946,1947,1949],{},[53,1948,554],{}," order with early return",": if a function returns early (via ",[53,1952,1953],{},"?"," or an explicit ",[53,1956,1957],{},"return","), locals still drop in reverse order at that return point — not at the end of the function's lexical block — which matters when destructors have side effects like releasing locks.",[31,1960,1961,1969,1970,742,1972,1975,1976,1978,1979,1981],{},[18,1962,1963,1964,569,1966],{},"Platform quirk — ",[53,1965,554],{},[53,1967,1968],{},"panic = \"abort\"",": if you set ",[53,1971,1968],{},[53,1973,1974],{},"Cargo.toml",", a panic during unwinding never runs remaining ",[53,1977,554],{}," impls in the current call stack (there is no unwinding at all), so RAII guards relying on ",[53,1980,554],{}," for cleanup won't fire on panic.",[23,1983,1985],{"id":1984},"spot-the-bug","🧠 Spot the Bug",[14,1987,1988],{},"What happens when this compiles and runs?",[86,1990,1991],{"language":88},[91,1992,1994],{"className":93,"code":1993,"language":88,"meta":95,"style":95},"struct Logger;\n\nimpl Drop for Logger {\n    fn drop(&mut self) {\n        println!(\"Logger dropped\");\n    }\n}\n\nfn make_and_swap() {\n    let mut first = Logger;\n    let second = Logger;\n    first = second;\n    println!(\"about to end scope\");\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    make_and_swap();\n}\n",[53,1995,1996,2005,2009,2022,2038,2050,2055,2059,2063,2072,2086,2099,2109,2120,2124,2128,2137,2145],{"__ignoreMap":95},[99,1997,1998,2000,2003],{"class":101,"line":102},[99,1999,603],{"class":105},[99,2001,2002],{"class":116}," Logger",[99,2004,254],{"class":109},[99,2006,2007],{"class":101,"line":140},[99,2008,